// What a transparency log is, in six ideas, each beside an artefact that webtessera produced
// while the site was built: a leaf hash, a tile, a signed checkpoint and its public key, an
// inclusion proof (as a Merkle tree and as an audit path) and a receipt. The artefacts come
// from two logs of the same entries under the same key (src/data/sample-log.ts,
// src/data/receipt-log.ts). The prose is hand-written; it states the design, and the guides and
// the reference say how to use it.

import "../../styles/artefacts.css";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";
import Doc from "../../layouts/Doc.astro";
import { docsSequence, meta, neighbours } from "../../lib/pages.ts";
import { renderReceipt } from "../../lib/receipt.ts";
import { getSiteData } from "../../lib/site.ts";
import { merkleTree, treeLeaves } from "../../lib/tree.ts";
import { href } from "../../lib/url.ts";
import { count, num } from "../../lib/words.ts";
import { html } from "../../shared/html.ts";
import { renderNoteBody } from "../../shared/note.ts";
import { renderProof } from "../../shared/proof.ts";
import { renderTile, TileWidth } from "../../shared/tiles.ts";

const target = 5;
const { prev, next } = neighbours(await docsSequence(), "docs/concepts/");
const { sample, receipt } = await getSiteData();
const { leaf, inclusion } = sample;
const tile = sample.tiles.find((t) => t.level === 0);
// The leaf figure: the entry's leaf hash, then the same entry with one byte changed, the changed
// byte and its hash set as tampering. Hashes are shortened to their first 12 bytes.
const leafLines = html`<span class="ln">hashLeaf("${leaf.text}")</span><span class="ln">  = ${leaf.hash.slice(0, 24)}…</span><span class="ln">hashLeaf("${leaf.altered.slice(0, leaf.at)}<span class="failed">${leaf.altered.slice(leaf.at)}</span>")</span><span class="ln">  = <span class="failed">${leaf.alteredHash.slice(0, 24)}…</span></span>`;
---

<Doc
	title={meta.concepts.title}
	description={meta.concepts.description}
	headings={[
		{ depth: 2, slug: "entries-become-leaves", text: "Entries become leaves" },
		{ depth: 2, slug: "leaves-fill-tiles", text: "Leaves fill tiles" },
		{ depth: 2, slug: "checkpoints-commit", text: "Checkpoints commit" },
		{ depth: 2, slug: "proofs-replace-trust", text: "Proofs replace trust" },
		{ depth: 2, slug: "receipts-travel", text: "Receipts travel" },
		{ depth: 2, slug: "witnesses-keep-it-honest", text: "Witnesses keep it honest" },
		{ depth: 2, slug: "specifications", text: "Specifications" },
	]}
	trail={[{ path: "docs/", title: meta.docs.title }]}
	prev={prev}
	next={next}
>
	<p class="lead" slot="lead">
		A transparency log is a tamper-evident record. Its operator can add entries but cannot remove, reorder or rewrite
		them unnoticed, because every reader can check two things alone: that an entry is in the log, and that today’s log
		extends yesterday’s. Certificate Transparency, the Go checksum database and Sigstore’s signature log all work this
		way.
	</p>
	<p slot="lead">
		The figures on this page are not drawings. webtessera made them while this site was built, from a log of
		{count(sample.entries.length, "entry", "entries")}: the names of the package’s entry points.
	</p>

	<h2 id="entries-become-leaves">Entries become leaves</h2>
	<p>
		Each entry is hashed into a leaf of a Merkle tree, and each pair of hashes into the node above them, up to a single
		root hash. A leaf and a node are hashed with different prefixes, so one cannot pass for the other. Change one byte of
		any entry and its leaf hash changes, then every hash on the way to the root.
	</p>
	<Figure caption="rfc6962.DefaultHasher" note={`entry ${leaf.index}, then one byte changed`}>
		<pre class="note" translate="no" set:html={leafLines.value} />
	</Figure>

	<h2 id="leaves-fill-tiles">Leaves fill tiles</h2>
	<p>
		The tree is stored as tiles: each tile holds {TileWidth} hashes of one level of the tree. A full tile never changes,
		and the tile at the log’s right edge is published as a partial tile, which is replaced by a longer one as entries
		arrive. The entries themselves are stored in bundles of {TileWidth} beside the tiles. Every tile and bundle has a
		fixed path, so a log is a directory of static files that any web server, bucket or CDN can serve, and caches can
		keep forever.
	</p>
	{tile !== undefined && <Fragment set:html={renderTile(tile, inclusion.index).value} />}
	<p>
		This partial tile holds the {num(sample.entries.length)} leaf hashes of the log, one cell per hash, toned by the
		hash; entry {inclusion.index} is outlined.
	</p>

	<h2 id="checkpoints-commit">Checkpoints commit</h2>
	<p>
		The log regularly signs its size and root hash. That signed checkpoint is a short text: the log’s name (its origin),
		the number of entries, the root hash, then one signature line per signer. Because the root depends on every entry,
		the signature commits the log to all of them, in order, for good.
	</p>
	<Figure caption="GET /checkpoint" note="signed while this page was built">
		<pre class="note labelled" translate="no" set:html={renderNoteBody(sample.note, true).value} />
	</Figure>
	<p>Anyone holding the log’s public key, its verifier key, can check that signature:</p>
	<Figure caption="vkey" note="the log’s public key">
		<pre class="note" translate="no"><span class="ln sig">{sample.vkey}</span></pre>
	</Figure>

	<h2 id="proofs-replace-trust">Proofs replace trust</h2>
	<p>
		A reader does not need the whole log to check it. An inclusion proof shows that an entry is in the tree under a
		checkpoint: it is the hashes of the subtrees beside the entry’s path to the root, and hashing them in order with the
		entry must give the checkpoint’s root.
	</p>
	<figure class="fig machine tree-fig">
		<figcaption><code>PATH({target}, D[{treeLeaves}])</code><span>RFC 6962, a tree of {num(treeLeaves)} entries</span></figcaption>
		<div class="tree-scroll" tabindex="0" role="group" aria-label="Merkle tree diagram" set:html={merkleTree(target).value} />
	</figure>
	<p>
		In the figure, entry {target} needs {num(Math.log2(treeLeaves))} hashes, not {num(treeLeaves)} entries. In the log
		this site built, proving that entry {inclusion.index} is in the tree of {inclusion.size} takes
		{count(inclusion.proof.length, "hash", "hashes")}; a log of a billion entries needs about {Math.ceil(Math.log2(1e9))}.
	</p>
	<Figure caption={`inclusionProof(${inclusion.index})`} note={`tree of ${inclusion.size}`}>
		<Fragment set:html={renderProof(inclusion).value} />
	</Figure>
	<p>
		A consistency proof shows that a newer checkpoint extends an older one, with nothing removed or changed. A client
		that keeps the last checkpoint it saw, and checks each new one this way, knows the log has only grown.
	</p>

	<h2 id="receipts-travel">Receipts travel</h2>
	<p>
		A receipt bundles an entry’s index, its inclusion proof and the checkpoint the proof is relative to. Whoever holds
		the receipt, the entry and the log’s public key can check it offline, without asking the log anything. webtessera’s
		<code>append()</code> returns one, verified, in the C2SP tlog-proof format.
	</p>
	<Figure
		caption={receipt.from === "append" ? "log.append(entry)" : `log.prove(${receipt.index})`}
		note="a receipt, as returned"
		class="receipt"
	>
		<pre class="note" translate="no" set:html={renderReceipt(receipt.text).value} />
		<p class="after">
			<code>verifyReceipt</code>: <span class="verified">entry {receipt.index} is in the tree of {receipt.size}.</span>
		</p>
	</Figure>

	<h2 id="witnesses-keep-it-honest">Witnesses keep it honest</h2>
	<p>
		A log could still show one history to some readers and another to the rest. Witnesses prevent that: they are
		independent services that check each new checkpoint is consistent with the last one they saw, and cosign it. A
		reader who requires cosignatures from enough witnesses knows that everyone sees the same log. A monitor goes
		further, and reads every entry.
	</p>

	<h2 id="specifications">Specifications</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6962#section-2.1">RFC 6962, section 2.1</a>: the Merkle tree, its hashes and its proofs.</li>
		<li><a href="https://c2sp.org/signed-note">C2SP signed-note</a> and <a href="https://c2sp.org/tlog-checkpoint">tlog-checkpoint</a>: the checkpoint’s text and signatures.</li>
		<li><a href="https://c2sp.org/tlog-tiles">C2SP tlog-tiles</a>: the tiles, the bundles and their paths.</li>
		<li><a href="https://c2sp.org/tlog-proof">C2SP tlog-proof</a>: the receipt.</li>
		<li><a href="https://c2sp.org/tlog-witness">C2SP tlog-witness</a> and <a href="https://c2sp.org/tlog-cosignature">tlog-cosignature</a>: witnesses and their cosignatures.</li>
		<li><a href="https://research.swtch.com/tlog">Transparent Logs for Skeptical Clients</a>, by Russ Cox: the design, explained from first principles.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		To see these pieces in a running log, <a href={href("#demo")}>append to the log on the home page</a>, then change an
		entry and watch its proof fail. To build one, start with <a href={href("docs/safe-api/")}>the safe API</a>.
	</p>
</Doc>

